  slide "AIPerf: process topology" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "One native binary; thread-per-core workers inside it"
    lede: "`aiperf profile` resolves Config v2 and re-executes the same `aiperf` binary as `--execute` over stdio. That single process owns scheduling, dataset, transport, and metrics as in-process modules — one native runtime, where Locust instead spreads work across separate worker processes. When `workers == 1` the sink co-locates on the coordinator thread; when `workers > 1` each OS thread is a self-contained sub-cell."

    narration: "One native binary re-execs itself for --execute. Workers are thread-per-core sub-cells inside that one process. Optional --cells adds a controller merge over Velo."

    term: {
      word: "execute process"
      meaning: "The same `aiperf` binary running in hidden `--execute` mode after `profile` projects Config v2 onto a protocol-v2 request over stdio. It is both the public CLI and the execution engine — scheduling, admission, transport sinks, and metrics live in-process. Parallelism is thread-per-core workers inside that one process."
    }

    points: [
      "`profile` → Config v2 → re-exec `aiperf --execute` over stdio; one native binary, no separate runner.",
      "Dataset, scheduling/admission, transport, and metrics stay in-process modules on one native runtime.",
      "`workers == 1`: co-located sink on the coordinator thread. `workers > 1`: each OS thread is a self-contained sub-cell.",
      "Optional `--cells N` promotes a controller that partitions work and merges cell results over Velo control traffic."
    ]

    caption: "One binary · in-process runtime · thread-per-core workers · optional cellular merge."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s5-hdr",
